---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/*
 * Photos for the "Outside this journal" section.
 */
const hobbyImages = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
    '../assets/hobbies/*.{jpg,jpeg,png,webp,avif}',
    { eager: true },
);

const hobbies = [
    { name: 'bouldering', caption: 'Bouldering' },
    { name: 'music', caption: 'Music' },
    { name: 'photography', caption: 'Photography' },
]
    .map((hobby) => ({
        ...hobby,
        image: Object.entries(hobbyImages).find(([path]) =>
            path.includes(`/${hobby.name}.`),
        )?.[1].default,
    }))
    .filter((hobby) => hobby.image);
---

<Base
    title="Luca's Journal"
    description="A public journal of projects, experiments, research, technical adventures, failed ideas, and the thoughts behind them."
>
    <h1>Hi, I'm Luca!</h1>

    <p>
        I'm currently 21, and I've been building things with computers since around 2019.
        My background is primarily in software, particularly C and C++, systems and
        low-level programming, performance-oriented software, and the parts of computing
        that sit close to the hardware. More recently, I've also been exploring digital
        hardware, RTL, ASIC design, EDA, and other fields such as mathematics, physics,
        and philosophy. In my day job I'm an AI engineer, working primarily on computer
        vision AI systems.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is my journal: a public record of the things I visit, build, investigate,
        break, abandon, finish, misunderstand, revisit, and learn from along the way.
    </p>

    <p>
        I want to begin by logging here that this journal was started on the 8th of August 2026.
    </p>

    <p class="cta">
        <a href="/entries/">Browse the entries &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <h2>What this is</h2>

    <p>
        This isn't meant to be a portfolio containing only polished work and successful
        projects. If anything, I want it to be the opposite.
    </p>

    <p>
        There will be finished projects here, but there will also be failed experiments,
        abandoned ideas, incomplete research, short investigations, strange technical
        detours, and things that seemed much more promising when I started them than
        they did by the time I stopped.
    </p>

    <p>
        Some entries may follow work that takes months. Others might come from an
        afternoon spent wondering how something works and following that question until
        I either find an answer or create several more questions. An idea I abandon
        today might return years from now when I have more knowledge, a better approach,
        or simply a reason to become interested in it again.
    </p>

    <p>
        More than anything, I want to document the process. Not just what I eventually
        built, but what I was thinking while building it: why I chose one approach over
        another, which assumptions turned out to be wrong, what failed, what surprised
        me, and how my understanding changed as I went.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this exists</h2>

    <p>
        I've been doing this kind of work since 2019, and a majority of it
        has already been lost to time.
    </p>

    <p>
        Old repositories disappeared. Experiments were left on computers I no longer
        use. Projects were abandoned without ever being properly documented. There are
        things I spent weeks or months working on that now survive mostly as fragments,
        old screenshots, a few files, or memories of having done them.
    </p>

    <p>
        I don't particularly like that.
    </p>

    <p>
        This journal is my attempt to keep a durable record of what I do from this point
        onward. I want to be able to look back years from now and see not only the things
        that worked, but the paths I took to get there and the ones that led nowhere.
    </p>

    <p>
        Making it public is deliberate as well. Keeping everything privately solves
        the archival problem, but it loses on what I find to be one of the most important
        parts of doing the work: other people can see it.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you find something interesting, notice that I've made a mistake, disagree
        with an assumption, know a better way of approaching something, or simply want
        to discuss an idea, I would like to hear about it. Feedback, criticism, corrections,
        suggestions, and questions are always welcome and encouraged.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I do the work</h2>

    <p>
        The work documented here is driven by personal curiosity.
    </p>

    <p>
        There isn't a curriculum behind it, a qualification I need to earn,
        or somebody telling me that I should work on a particular problem. Most things
        start because I encounter something I don't understand, something I think could
        be better, or more simply: something that seems interesting enough that
        I want to know what happens if I keep pulling on the thread.
    </p>

    <p>
        Sometimes I spend a few days on it, learn enough to satisfy the original
        curiosity, and move on. Sometimes it may simply fail completely. Other
        times, it may become a serious project, research, or something else entirely.
    </p>

    <p>
        I don't really want to constrain that curiosity to a particular subject either.
        My strongest background is in software, especially C and C++, systems programming,
        low-level and performance-oriented work. But I've interacted with a much more
        extensive range of software than those areas alone, and if something interests
        me enough to investigate it, there is a reasonable chance it will eventually
        appear here.
    </p>

    <p>
        That increasingly includes hardware and other fields as well. I've been
        learning digital design, RTL, synthesis, physical design, and ASIC development,
        while also becoming more interested in mathematics, physics, and philosophy.
        I expect some entries to involve exploring problems and ideas in those fields,
        even where they fall well outside my established background. I still consider
        myself a beginner in hardware, and very much a learner in mathematics, physics,
        and philosophy, so I expect my understanding of all of these areas to evolve
        over time.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on expertise</h2>

    <p>
        I explore a lot of subjects, and I am not an expert in many of them.
    </p>

    <p>
        In particular, this journal will inevitably take me into mathematics, physics,
        electronics, hardware, and other areas where my knowledge may be much less
        developed than my software background. I am not a mathematician or a physicist,
        and I don't want the existence of a nicely formatted page to imply otherwise.
    </p>

    <p>
        Many entries are likely to document me learning something rather than explaining
        something I have already mastered. There will probably be mistakes, bad
        assumptions, misunderstandings, awkward derivations, and occasions where I take
        a very long route toward something that would be obvious to somebody with more
        experience.
    </p>

    <p>
        That's fine. It's part of what I want this journal to preserve,
        and it's part of what I consider a healthy progression.
    </p>

    <p>
        If I later discover that something I wrote was wrong, I'd rather come back,
        explain what was wrong, and correct it than quietly erase the original thought
        process. One of the things I hope this journal eventually captures is how my
        understanding changes over time.
    </p>

    <p>
        So where I venture outside the areas I know well, please read the entries as
        records of exploration rather than claims of authority. And if you know more
        than I do and see something wrong, please tell me.
    </p>

    <h2>How I use AI</h2>

    <p>
        I regularly make use of AI in my work, and I think that is worth being
        transparent about from the start.
    </p>

    <p>
        I use it primarily as a tool for efficiency: writing boilerplate, producing
        quick proof-of-concepts, exploring unfamiliar areas, checking ideas, and
        generally reducing time spent on work that is not itself the point of what
        I'm trying to do.
    </p>

    <p>
        The thoughts, experiences, decisions, conclusions, and learning documented
        here are still my own. Anything I consider important enough to publish,
        commit to, or present as my work is something I expect myself to understand,
        scrutinize, revise, and ultimately take responsibility for.
    </p>

    <p>
        I don't see AI as a substitute for that process. If anything, I find the
        value of the work comes from deciding what is worth pursuing, understanding
        why something works or fails, and being willing to spend the time necessary
        to develop that understanding.
    </p>

    <h2>Outside this journal</h2>

    <p>
        Computers occupy a large part of my time, but they're far from the only thing
        I'm interested in.
    </p>

    <p>
        I also make music, boulder, powerlift, DJ, play chess, do photography, and regularly
        find other things to become interested in. Most of that won't appear here because
        this isn't intended to be a general diary of my life.
    </p>

    {
        hobbies.length > 0 && (
            <>
                <div class="hobby-photos">
                    {hobbies.map((hobby) => (
                        <button
                            type="button"
                            class="photo-button"
                            aria-label={`View ${hobby.caption} photo full screen`}
                        >
                            <Image
                                src={hobby.image!}
                                alt={hobby.caption}
                                widths={[480, 960]}
                                sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 33vw"
                            />
                        </button>
                    ))}
                </div>
                <dialog class="lightbox" aria-label="Full screen photo">
                    <img src="" alt="" />
                </dialog>
            </>
        )
    }

    <p>
        Those interests do, however, influence what I work on. A software project might
        begin because of music. A graphics experiment might grow out of photography.
        A hardware idea might exist because I wanted to understand a system I was using
        somewhere else. The boundary between interests is rarely particularly clean,
        so some of that will inevitably leak into the work documented here.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you've read this far — thank you for reading what I have to say!
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing</h2>

    <p>
        I want the material here to be useful beyond simply being read.
    </p>

    <p>
        The source code for this website is licensed under the MIT License.
        Unless otherwise stated, my original journal entries, research notes, diagrams,
        figures, and other original content are licensed under the Creative Commons
        Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0).
    </p>

    <p>
        That means you're welcome to share, reference, adapt, and build upon my original
        content, including for commercial purposes, provided that appropriate credit is
        given and modifications are identified where required.
    </p>

    <p>
        Third-party code, images, assets, quotations, and other material remain subject
        to their respective licenses and copyrights.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>

    <p>
        If you want to discuss an entry, point out a mistake, suggest something I should
        look into, share related work, or just talk about something you found interesting,
        feel free to get in touch.
    </p>

    <ul class="contact">
        <li>
            <a href="mailto:luca.goddijn@gmail.com">Email</a>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/Arty3">GitHub</a>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/luca-goddijn/">LinkedIn</a>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta bottom-cta">
        <a href="/entries/">Browse the journal &rarr;</a>
        <br />
        <a href="https://github.com/Arty3/journal">Browse the repository &rarr;</a>
    </p>
</Base>

<style>
    .cta {
        margin-top: 2rem;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .bottom-cta {
        margin-top: 3rem;
    }

    .contact {
        padding-left: 1.25rem;
    }

    .hobby-photos {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
        gap: 1rem;
        margin: 2rem 0;
    }

    .photo-button {
        position: relative;
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        padding: 0;
        border: none;
        background: none;
        border-radius: 10px;
        cursor: zoom-in;
        transition: transform 0.25s ease;
    }

    .photo-button:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    /*
     * The whole tile grows over the surrounding content: transforms
     * don't participate in layout, so nothing else moves.
     */
    .photo-button:hover,
    .photo-button:focus-visible {
        transform: scale(1.06);
        z-index: 1;
    }

    .hobby-photos img {
        width: 100%;
        aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
        object-fit: cover;
        display: block;
        border-radius: 10px;
        filter: saturate(1.2);
    }

    .lightbox {
        padding: 0;
        border: none;
        background: transparent;
        max-width: 92vw;
        max-height: 92vh;
        cursor: zoom-out;
    }

    .lightbox::backdrop {
        background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
    }

    .lightbox img {
        display: block;
        /* fixed viewport-sized box: contain scales any aspect ratio
           up or down to fill it, so wide images open large too */
        width: 92vw;
        height: 92vh;
        object-fit: contain;
        filter: saturate(1.2);
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
        .hobby-photos {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        }
    }
</style>

<script>
    const lightbox = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('.lightbox');
    const lightboxImg = lightbox?.querySelector('img');

    if (lightbox && lightboxImg) {
        for (const button of document.querySelectorAll('.photo-button')) {
            button.addEventListener('click', () => {
                const img = button.querySelector('img');
                if (!img) return;
                /* src (not currentSrc) is the full-resolution version,
                   so the lightbox can fill the screen without blurring */
                lightboxImg.src = img.src;
                lightboxImg.alt = img.alt;
                lightbox.showModal();
            });
        }

        /* click anywhere (image or backdrop) closes; Esc works natively */
        lightbox.addEventListener('click', () => lightbox.close());
    }
</script>
